import Agents.World.Tally;
import Agents.World.Goods;
program AgentsExe;

// WHAT A THING COSTS, OR WHAT A PLACE HOLDS -- how much of each good.
//
// `typealias` and not a class of its own, which the building layout asks for by name: the alias is
// TRANSPARENT, so `Bill` and `Tally<Goods>` are one type and a method declared with either accepts
// the other. What it buys is the reading. `Bill owed = site.short();` says what the value means;
// `Tally<Goods> owed = ...` says how it is stored, ninety times over.
//
// `Goods` is the tag because the goods are the one family here with no enum of their own: they are
// a global index across four family enums (Nothing, then Extracted, Processed, Manufactured,
// Industrial), and `Goods` is the catalogue that derives it. A tally of crafts is `Tally<Craft>`
// and the compiler will not let one stand in for the other.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace World {

        public typealias Bill = Tally<Goods>;
    }
}
